# Python

## Version and tooling

- Target Python 3.11+. Use modern syntax: `match`, `X | Y` unions, PEP 604.
- Manage dependencies with `uv` and a lockfile. Do not invent
  `requirements.txt` files when a `pyproject.toml` already exists.
- Format and lint with `ruff`. Type-check with `pyright` or `mypy`.

## Type hints

- Required on every public function, method, and class attribute.
- Use built-in generics: `list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`, `tuple[int, ...]`.
- Prefer `TypeAlias`, `TypedDict`, and `Protocol` over `Any`.
- `Any` needs a comment explaining why it is unavoidable.

## Function shape

- One responsibility per function. Aim for under 20 lines.
- Maximum 5 positional parameters; promote to a dataclass past that.
- Return one type. `Optional[T]` is fine; `Union[A, B, C]` usually means
  the function is doing too much.
- Verb-noun naming: `calculate_total`, `fetch_user`, `validate_input`.
- Boolean returns start with `is_`, `has_`, `can_`, `should_`.

## Error handling

- Catch the narrowest exception that fits. Never bare `except:`.
- Re-raise with context: `raise NewError("...") from e`.
- Build domain exception hierarchies; do not abuse `ValueError` for
  everything.
- Validate inputs at the boundary of the system, fail fast inside.

## Imports

- Standard library, then third-party, then local. Blank line between groups.
- Sorted alphabetically within each group (ruff handles this).
- No wildcard imports.
- No imports inside functions unless avoiding a true circular import.

## Data structures

- Prefer immutable: `tuple` over `list` for fixed sequences,
  `frozenset` over `set` when possible.
- Use `dataclasses` for structured mutable data, `NamedTuple` for
  immutable structured data.
- Reach for `collections.Counter`, `collections.defaultdict`,
  `collections.deque` before reinventing them.

## Performance

- Profile before optimizing. Do not pre-optimize.
- Use generators for large sequences.
- Cache expensive pure functions with `functools.cache`.
- Use `__slots__` only when memory is measurably tight.

## Async

- Use `async def` only when there is real I/O concurrency to gain.
- Never block the event loop with `time.sleep`, sync `requests`, or CPU-
  heavy work. Use `asyncio.sleep`, `httpx.AsyncClient`, or `run_in_executor`.

## Anti-patterns

- Mutable default arguments: `def f(x=[])` → use `None` and assign inside.
- Catching `Exception` to "make the test pass".
- Returning `None` to signal an error path; raise instead.
- `print()` for debugging in production code; use the `logging` module.
- Reaching for inheritance when composition is enough.
